Developer's Guide
=================

Installation
--------------

To install bacco:

$ pip install -e . [--user]

This ensures that the C-routines are recompiled if necessary.

Note that this and import of bacco from python requires that you have the gsl and fftw
library loaded ("module load GSL" and "module load FFTW" on atlas)

If you would like to use bacco with anaconda and jupyterlab you might want to have
a look at docs/minicondainstall.sh

To upgrade to numpy 2, you might need to regenerate the _cmock.c. To do this just delete it and 
reinstall bacco:
>> rm bacco/cyengine/_cmock.c

Additional packages
---------- --------

- setuptool

>> pip install setuptools --user

- CAMB

>> wget https://github.com/cmbant/CAMB/archive/0.1.7.tar.gz
>> tar xvzf 0.1.7.tar.gz
>> cd CAMB-0.1.7/pycamb
>> python setup.py install [--user]

- CLASS

>> git clone https://github.com/lesgourg/class_public
>> cd class_public

In 'python/classy.pyx' add:

+@cython.auto_pickle(False)
 cdef class Class:

Optional (solve an increase of 30kb per call to Class (not removable with clean()):
@@ -137,6 +137,9 @@ cdef class Class:
        return True

    def empty(self):
+        free(self.fc.name)
+        free(self.fc.value)
+        free(self.fc.read)
        self._pars = {}
        self.ready = False
 
>> make

- Corrfunc

>> git clone https://github.com/manodeep/Corrfunc.git
>> cd Corrfunc
>> make install
If this fail, try changin common.mk to:
PYTHON_CONFIG_EXE:= /location/of/python-config
/scratch/scicomp/easybuild/CentOS/7.3.1611/Haswell/software/Python/2.7.14-foss-2017b/bin/python-config
for atlas
>> python setup.py install [--user]
Also, Corrfunct may need wurlitzer:
>> pip install wurlitzer --user

- L-Galaxies

>> wget http://galformod.mpa-garching.mpg.de/public/LGalaxies/run_model/SpecPhotTables.tar

- EuclidEmulator

  >> git clone https://github.com/miknab/EuclidEmulator
  >> cd EuclidEmulator/wrapper2/
  in CMakeLists.txt:
  >> set( PYTHON_MODULE_PATH "<installation-dir>" )
  >> export PYTHONPATH="<installation-dir>"
  >> mkdir build && cd build && cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=<installation-dir> .. && make && make install



Commits and Testing
-------------------

Before every commit, we should execute py.test:

$ py.test --quick

from time to time and every time a new function/feature is added, we should run the
full test suite

$ py.test --model=[LCDM, nuLCDM, wCDM]


Coding Style
--------------

We should try to comply with PEP8 coding style

http://docs.python-guide.org/en/latest/writing/style/

We can check our code complies by using (pip install pycodestyle)

> pycodestyle mycode.py

or try to change automatically with

> autopep8 --in-place optparse.py


Running bacco in clusters
-------------------------

The suggested method for running bacco in clusters is installing it
in a python virtual environment, that can be activated in the submission
script. This eliminates the need to export all python library/module paths,
otherwise needed for running.

To this purpose the command

> virtualenv YourEnv --python=/path/to/your/python

will create a new virtual environment.

On atlas, this should be done in /scratch/username, in order to be able to
run scripts. Moreover, if you loaded the module "Python/2.7.14-foss-2017b",
you should use
--python=/scratch/scicomp/easybuild/CentOS/7.3.1611/Haswell/software/Python/2.7.14-foss-2017b/bin/python

You activate your virtual environment with

> source YourEnv/bin/activate

and you deactivate it just by typing

> deactivate

Once you have installed bacco in the virtual environment in scratch, you can add
to your job submission script

> ...[PBS options]...
>
> module purge
> module load ...
> module load Python/2.7.14-foss-2017b
>
> source /path/to/venv/bin/activate
> python your_script.py
> deactivate


